== THE FIFTH STAGE

Now I saw in my dream that they went forward until they had come to the brow of the Hill; where Piety, bethinking herself, cried out, Alas, I have forgotten what I intended to bestow on Christiana and her companions: I will go back and fetch it. So she ran and fetched it. While she was gone, Christiana thought she heard, in a grove a little way off on the right hand, a most exquisite melodious note, with words much like these:

#quote[“Through all my life thy favor is \
So frankly showed to me, \
That in thy House for evermore \
My dwelling-place shall be.”]

And still listening, she thought she heard another answer it, saying,

#quote[“For why? The Lord our God is good; \
His mercy is forever sure; \
His truth at all times firmly stood, \
And shall from age to age endure.”]

So Christiana asked Prudence who it was that made those exquisite notes. Song 2:11, 12. They are, answered she, our country birds: they sing these notes only seldom, except in the spring, when the flowers appear, and the sun shines warm, and then you may hear them all day long. I often, said she, go out to hear them; we also often keep them tame in our house. They are very fine company for us when we are melancholy: also they make the woods, and groves, and solitary places, places desirable to be in.

By this time Piety had come back. So she said to Christiana, Look here, I have brought you a sketch of all those things that you have seen at our house, upon which you may look when you find yourself forgetful, and call those things to remembrance again for your edification and comfort.

Now they began to go down the hill into the Valley of Humiliation. It was a steep hill, and the way was slippery; but they were very careful; so they got down pretty well. When they were down in the valley, Piety said to Christiana, This is the place where Christian your husband met with the foul fiend Apollyon, and where they had that dreadful fight that they had: I know you cannot but have heard of it. But be of good courage; as long as you have Mr. Great-Heart here to be your guide and conductor, we hope you will fare the better. So when these two had committed the pilgrims to the care of their guide, he went forward, and they went after.

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: Then said Mr. Great-Heart, We need not be so afraid of this valley, for there is nothing here to hurt us, unless we bring it on ourselves. It is true, Christian did meet with Apollyon here, with whom he also had a severe combat: but that fray was the fruit of those slips that he got in his going down the hill: for those who get slips there must look for combats here. And that is why this valley has got so hard a name. For the common people, when they hear that some frightful thing has befallen such a one in such a place, are of the opinion that that place is haunted with some foul fiend, or evil spirit; when, alas! it is for the fruit of their doing that such things befall them there. This Valley of Humiliation is of itself as fruitful a place as any the crow flies over; and I am persuaded, if we could hit upon it, we might find somewhere hereabouts something that might give us an account of why Christian was so hard beset in this place.]

Then James said to his mother, Look, yonder stands a pillar, and it looks as if something was written on it; let us go and see what it is. So they went and found written there, “Let Christian’s slips, before he came here, and the battles that he met with in this place, be a warning to those who come after.” Look, said their guide, did I not tell you that there was something hereabouts that would give a hint of the reason why Christian was so hard beset in this place? Then turning to Christiana, he said, This is no disparagement to Christian, any more than to any others whose fortune and lot it was. For it is easier going up than down this hill, and that can be said of only a few hills in all these parts of the world. But we will leave the good man; he is at rest: he also had a brave victory over his enemy. May He who dwells above grant that we fare no worse, when we come to be tried, than he.

But we will come again to this Valley of Humiliation. It is the best and most fruitful piece of ground in all those parts. It is fat ground, and, as you see, consists largely of meadows; and if a man were to come here in the summertime, as we do now, if he did not know anything about it before, and if he also delighted himself in the sight of his eyes, he might see that which would be delightful to him. See how green this valley is; also how beautified with lilies. Song 2:1. I have known many laboring men who have got good estates in this Valley of Humiliation; for God resists the proud, but gives grace to the humble. Jas 4:6; 1 Pet 5:5. Indeed it is a very fruitful soil, and brings forth by handfuls. Some also have wished that the nearest way to their Father’s house were here, that they might be troubled no more with either hills or mountains to go over; but the way is the way, and there is an end.

Now, as they were going along, and talking, they spied a boy feeding his father’s sheep. The boy was in very poor clothes, but of a very fresh and well-favored countenance; and as he sat by himself, he sang. Hark, said Mr. Great-Heart, to what the shepherd’s boy says. So they listened, and he said,

#quote[“He that is down, needs fear no fall; \
He that is low, no pride: \
He that is humble, ever shall \
Have God to be his guide.]

#quote[I am content with what I have, \
Little be it or much; \
And, Lord, contentment still I crave, \
Because thou savest such.]

#quote[Fulness to such, a burden is, \
That go on pilgrimage; \
Here little, and hereafter bliss, \
Is best from Age to Age.”]

Then said the guide, Do you hear him? I will dare to say that this boy lives a merrier life, and wears more of that herb called heart’s-ease in his bosom, than he who is clad in silk and velvet. But we will proceed in our discourse.

In this valley our Lord formerly had his country-house: he loved much to be here. He also loved to walk these meadows, for he found the air was pleasant. Besides, here a man will be free from the noise, and from the hurryings of this life: all states are full of noise and confusion; only the Valley of Humiliation is that empty and solitary place. Here a man will not be so hindered and held back in his contemplation as he is apt to be in other places. This is a valley that nobody walks in but those who love a pilgrim’s life. And though Christian had the hard lot to meet with Apollyon here, and to enter into a brisk encounter with him, yet I must tell you that in former times men have met with angels here, Hos 12:4, 5, have found pearls here, Matt 13:46, and have in this place found the words of life. Prov 8:35.

Did I say our Lord had his country-house here in former days, and that he loved to walk here? I will add—in this place, and to the people who love and walk these grounds, he has left a yearly income, to be faithfully paid them at certain seasons, for their maintenance on the way, and for their further encouragement to go on in their pilgrimage.

#speech[#smallcaps[Samuel]: Now, as they went on, Samuel said to Mr. Great-Heart, Sir, I perceive that in this valley my father and Apollyon had their battle; but whereabouts was the fight? for I perceive this valley is large.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: Your father had the battle with Apollyon at a place yonder before us, in a narrow passage, just beyond Forgetful Green. And indeed that place is the most dangerous place in all these parts. For if at any time pilgrims meet with any hard assault, it is when they forget what favors they have received, and how unworthy they are of them. This is the place also where others have been hard put to it. But more of the place when we have come to it; for I am persuaded that to this day there remains either some sign of the battle, or some monument to testify that such a battle was fought there.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mercy]: Then said Mercy, I think I am as well in this valley as I have been anywhere else in all our journey: the place, I think, suits my spirit. I love to be in such places, where there is no rattling of coaches, nor rumbling of wheels. I think here one may, without much disturbance, be thinking what he is, where he came from, what he has done, and to what the King has called him. Here one may think, and break at heart, and melt in one’s spirit, until one’s eyes become like the fish-pools in Heshbon. Song 7:4. Those who go rightly through this valley of Baca make it a well; the rain that God sends down from heaven upon those who are here also fills the pools. This valley is also the one from which the King will give to his own their vineyards; and those who go through it shall sing, as Christian did, for all that he met with Apollyon. Ps 84:5–7; Hos 2:15.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: It is true, said their guide; I have gone through this valley many a time, and was never better than when here. I have also been a guide to several pilgrims, and they have confessed the same. “This is the one I will esteem,” says the King, “he who is humble and contrite in spirit, who trembles at My word.” Isa 66:2.]

Now they had come to the place where the aforementioned battle was fought. Then the guide said to Christiana, her children, and Mercy, This is the place; on this ground Christian stood, and up there came Apollyon against him; and look. And, look, did I not tell you? here is some of your husband’s blood upon these stones to this day: see, also, how here and there some of the splinters of Apollyon’s broken darts are still to be seen upon the place. See, also, how they beat the ground with their feet as they fought, to make good their places against each other; how also with their by-blows they split the very stones in pieces. Truly, Christian played the man here, and showed himself as stout as Hercules could, had he been there, even he himself. When Apollyon was beaten, he made his retreat to the next valley, that is called the Valley of the Shadow of Death, to which we shall come soon. Look, yonder also stands a monument, on which is engraved this battle, and Christian’s victory, to his fame, throughout all ages. So because it stood just on the wayside before them, they stepped to it, and read the writing, which word for word was this:

#quote[“Hard by here was a battle fought, \
Most strange, and yet most true; \
Christian and Apollyon fought \
Each other to subdue.]

#quote[The man so bravely play’d the man, \
He made the fiend to fly; \
Of which a monument I stand, \
The same to testify.”]

When they had passed by this place, they came upon the borders of the Shadow of Death. This Valley was longer than the other; a place also most strangely haunted with evil things, as many are able to testify: but these women and children went through it the better, because they had daylight, and because Mr. Great-Heart was their conductor.

When they were entering upon this valley, they thought they heard a groaning, as of dying men; a very great groaning. They also thought that they heard words of lamentation, spoken as by some in extreme torment. These things made the boys quake; the women also looked pale and wan; but their guide told them to be of good comfort.

So they went on a little further, and they thought that they felt the ground begin to shake under them, as if there were some hollow place there: they also heard a kind of hissing, as of serpents, but nothing had yet appeared. Then the boys said, Are we not yet at the end of this doleful place? But the guide also told them to be of good courage, and look well to their feet; in case, said he, you are caught in some snare.

Now James began to be sick; but I think the cause of it was fear: so his mother gave him some of that glass of spirits that had been given her at the Interpreter’s house, and three of the pills that Mr. Skill had prepared, and the boy began to revive. So they went on till they came to about the middle of the valley; and then Christiana said, I think I see something yonder on the road before us, a thing of such a shape as I have not seen. Then Joseph said, Mother, what is it? An ugly thing, child; an ugly thing, said she. But, mother, what is it like? said he. It is like I cannot tell what, said she; and now it is only a little way off. Then she said, It is near.

Well, said Mr. Great-Heart, let those who are most afraid keep close to me. So the fiend came on, and the conductor met it; but when it had come to him, it vanished from all their sight. Then they remembered what had been said some time ago: “Resist the devil, and he will flee from you.” Jas 4:7.

So they went on, being a little refreshed. But they had not gone far before Mercy, looking behind her, saw, as she thought, something most like a lion, and it came at a great padding pace after them: and it had a hollow voice of roaring; and at every roar it gave, it made the valley echo, and all their hearts ache, except the heart of him who was their guide. So it came up, and Mr. Great-Heart went behind, and put the pilgrims all before him. The lion also came on quickly, and Mr. Great-Heart set himself to give him battle. 1 Pet 5:8, 9. But when the lion saw that it was determined that resistance should be made, he also drew back, and came no further.

Then they went on again, and their conductor went before them, till they came to a place where a pit was cast up the whole breadth of the way; and before they could be prepared to go over that, a great mist and a darkness fell upon them, so that they could not see. Then the pilgrims said, Alas! what shall we do now? But their guide answered, Fear not; stand still, and see what an end will be put to this also; so they stayed there, because their path was marred. Then they also thought that they heard more plainly the noise and rushing of the enemies; the fire also and the smoke of the pit were much easier to discern. Then Christiana said to Mercy, Now I see what my poor husband went through. I have heard much of this place, but I was never here before now. Poor man! he went here all alone in the night; he had night almost the whole way through: also these fiends were busy about him, as if they would have torn him in pieces. Many have spoken of it; but none can tell what the Valley of the Shadow of Death should mean until they come into it themselves. The heart knows its own bitterness; and no stranger shares in its joy. Prov 14:10. To be here is a fearful thing.

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: This is like doing business in great waters, or like going down into the deep. This is like being in the heart of the sea, and like going down to the bottoms of the mountains. Now it seems as if the earth, with its bars, were about us forever. But let those who walk in darkness, and have no light, trust in the name of the Lord, and lean upon their God. Isa 50:10. For my part, as I have told you already, I have often gone through this valley, and have been much harder put to it than I am now: and yet you see I am alive. I would not boast, for I am not my own savior; but I trust we shall have a good deliverance. Come, let us pray for light to Him who can lighten our darkness, and who can rebuke not only these, but all the Satans in hell.]

So they cried and prayed, and God sent light and deliverance, for there was now no hindrance in their way; no, not there where just now they had been stopped by a pit. Yet they had not got through the valley. So they still went on, and met with great stinks and loathsome smells, to their great annoyance. Then Mercy said to Christiana, It is not so pleasant being here as at the gate, or at the Interpreter’s, or at the house where we lay last.

Oh but, said one of the boys, it is not so bad to go through here as it is to abide here always; and for all I know, one reason why we must go this way to the house prepared for us is that our home might be the sweeter to us.

Well said, Samuel, said the guide; you have now spoken like a man. Why, if ever I get out of here again, said the boy, I think I shall prize light and good way better than I ever did in all my life. Then the guide said, We shall be out by and by.

So on they went, and Joseph said, Cannot we see to the end of this valley yet? Then the guide said, Look to your feet, for we shall soon be among the snares: so they looked to their feet, and went on; but they were much troubled with the snares. Now, when they had come among the snares, they spied a man cast into the ditch on the left hand, with his flesh all rent and torn. Then the guide said, That is one Heedless, who was going this way: he has lain there a great while. There was one Take-Heed with him when he was taken and slain, but he escaped their hands. You cannot imagine how many are killed hereabouts, and yet men are so foolishly venturesome as to set out lightly on pilgrimage, and to come without a guide. Poor Christian! it was a wonder that he escaped here; but he was beloved of his God: also he had a good heart of his own, or else he could never have done it.

Now they drew toward the end of this way; and just there where Christian had seen the cave when he went by, out of it came Maul, a giant. This Maul used to prey on young pilgrims with sophistry; and he called Great-Heart by his name, and said to him, How many times have you been forbidden to do these things? Then said Mr. Great-Heart, What things? What things! said the giant; you know what things: but I will put an end to your trade.

But, please, said Mr. Great-Heart, before we fall to it, let us understand why we must fight. Now the women and children stood trembling, and did not know what to do. Said the giant, You rob the country, and rob it with the worst of thefts. These are only generalities, said Mr. Great-Heart; come to particulars, man.

Then the giant said, You practice the craft of a kidnapper; you gather up women and children, and carry them into a strange country, to the weakening of my master’s kingdom. But now Great-Heart replied, I am a servant of the God of heaven; my business is to persuade sinners to repentance. I am commanded to do my best to turn men, women, and children from darkness to light, and from the power of Satan to God; and if this is indeed the ground of your quarrel, let us fall to it as soon as you like.

Then the giant came up, and Mr. Great-Heart went to meet him; and as he went he drew his sword, but the giant had a club. So without more ado they fell to it, and at the first blow the giant struck Mr. Great-Heart down upon one of his knees. With that the women and children cried out. So Mr. Great-Heart, recovering himself, laid about him in full and vigorous manner, and gave the giant a wound in his arm. So he fought for the space of an hour, to such a height of heat that the breath came out of the giant’s nostrils as the heat does out of a boiling cauldron.

Then they sat down to rest; but Mr. Great-Heart gave himself to prayer. Also the women and children did nothing but sigh and cry all the time that the battle lasted.

When they had rested, and taken breath, they both fell to it again; and Mr. Great-Heart, with a blow, fetched the giant down to the ground. No, hold, let me recover, said he: so Mr. Great-Heart fairly let him get up. So to it they went again, and the giant came within very little of breaking Mr. Great-Heart’s skull with his club.

Mr. Great-Heart, seeing that, runs to him in the full heat of his spirit, and pierces him under the fifth rib. With that the giant began to faint, and could hold up his club no longer. Then Mr. Great-Heart seconded his blow, and struck the head of the giant from his shoulders. Then the women and children rejoiced, and Mr. Great-Heart also praised God for the deliverance he had worked.

When this was done, they together erected a pillar, and fastened the giant’s head on it, and wrote under it in letters that passers-by might read,

#quote[“He that did wear this head was one \
That pilgrims did misuse; \
He stopped their way, he spared none, \
But did them all abuse; \
Until that I Great-Heart arose, \
The pilgrims’ guide to be; \
Until that I did him oppose \
That was their enemy.”]
